WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.970
By the 1890s, hunting had left perhaps
just 20 to 100 northern elephant seals

2
00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:07.060
alive.

3
00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:11.010
Today there are well over 100,000 of them.

4
00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:15.480
Yet so many seals carry very little
genetic variation between them.

5
00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:20.540
So why do small, isolated populations end
up so genetically different from everyone

6
00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:21.640
else?

7
00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:26.100
In this mission, you'll track how allele
frequencies in a population change over

8
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:27.880
many generations.

9
00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.150
Then you'll see what happens when a river,

10
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.100
mountains or distance
split a population in two.

11
00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:40.120
Your mission: explain why small, isolated
populations become so genetically different.
